  "headline": "The front end breaks first: I own the 10-year with the dollar and start buying gold",
  "body": [
    "This is a panic pricing a Fed that bends. Stocks fell 3.7% on the day and the VIX closed at 35.9, the top of its year's range. But the bid went to the front of the curve, not the back: the 5-year fell 17 basis points against 10 for the 30-year. That is the market demanding cuts and the long end declining to follow all the way down.",
    "Yesterday I said that if stocks fell another 5% with the 30-year rallying, the long bond was a haven and I would own the 10-year. Stocks fell short of the letter, but the 30-year rallied anyway, and I am not hiding behind a number when the direction is this clear. My gold buy condition, a higher close with the VIX also higher, was met today: up 0.15% to $3,539 while nearly everything else sold.",
    "The curve is doing what fiscal dominance says it should, from the other side. The 30-year minus 5-year spread is 60 basis points, 14 wider over the month, because the front end is collapsing toward an accommodating Fed, not because the long end is being dumped. MOVE at 106, near the top of its range, says the bond market has stopped being calm about it; my retraction trigger was a spread under 45 with MOVE below 90, and the market is moving away from both. Bitcoin is the loser, down 5.8% to a fresh low, and it buys 19% fewer ounces of gold than a month ago: a risk asset here, not a hedge, and I want none of it until that ratio turns.",
    "Drop Site reports the Pentagon's Iran war costs top $48 billion and that Saudi Arabia struck Hodeidah port, with Ansarallah vowing a response. Brent fell 2.3% into that and the rupee gained 0.9% on the day: oil importers are being paid, not punished. The war's channel into my markets is fiscal rather than energy right now, and the long end's lag behind the front end is the only place it shows.",
    "I own the dollar and now the 10-year at 4.91%, and I start buying gold here, early as usual. I stay out of bitcoin, Brent and copper, and I do not own the 30-year yet: at 5.30% it is the maturity the Treasury has to sell into this, and its lag is the thesis. I am wrong on the 10-year if it is back above 5.15% with stocks still falling, because that is the fiscal signature returning. I am wrong on gold if it loses $3,450 with the VIX still rising. Next I watch the 30-year minus 5-year spread: through 70 basis points on a cut repricing means the long bond is being financed at the market's price, which is where I started."
  ],
